    {
      "question": "e-5293",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "ff64e839e917fe598edc48b387426fd435923c1a087f27b58b20d12d8f84e883",
      "new_sha256": "209d6d3e2605966eb65e3e1e6de9848fd82ca50dc8b891aade5f11d71ef7d8a2",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-5293",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "First, we divide both sides of the equation by $1+\\textrm{i}$ to isolate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3+5\\text{i} & =(p+\\text{i}q)(1+\\text{i}) \\\\ \\frac{3+5\\text{i}}{1+\\text{i}} & =p+\\text{i}q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to simplify the fraction on the left-hand side, we multiply the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(3+5\\text{i})(1-\\text{i})}{(1+\\text{i})(1-\\text{i})} & =p+\\text{i}q \\\\ \\frac{3-3\\text{i}+5\\text{i}-5{\\text{i}}^{2}}{{1}^{2}-{\\text{i}}^{2}} & =p+\\text{i}q \\\\ \\frac{3+2\\text{i}-5(-1)}{1-(-1)} & =p+\\text{i}q \\\\ \\frac{8+2\\text{i}}{2} & =p+\\text{i}q \\\\ 4+\\text{i} & =p+\\text{i}q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can equate the real parts and the imaginary parts separately.\n\n- Equating the real parts, we get $4=p.$\n\n- Equating the imaginary parts, we get $1=q.$\n\nTherefore, $p=4$ and $q=1.$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041873/example-5293.json",
          "file_sha256": "9d747b6b200afde165ecf40afb6fc21c7d0734fb9467407ca5ef6e0ad72183fa"
        }
